# Code review standards

Orchestration loop: [`.cursor/rules/orchestration.mdc`](orchestration.mdc). This rule covers **review quality and delegation**, not the full harness loop.

## Purpose

Keep quality, security, and maintainability high before commits and merges. Use **subagents** (`.cursor/agents/`) for focused passes—not inline chat pretending to be independent review.

**Independence:** Each harness review file under `docs/project/feedback/<n>/` is **authored by one subagent**. The **generator** must not pre-fill reviewer files.

## When review is mandatory

- After substantive application code changes (not doc-only typos).
- Before pushing to shared branches or opening/merging PRs.
- When touching **security-sensitive** areas: auth, sessions, payments, PII, uploads, webhooks.
- When making **architectural** or **data-layer** changes (call **architect** for design; still run code/security/DB review as applicable).

## Quick checklist

- Readable names; focused functions (prefer under ~50 lines); cohesive files (prefer under ~800 lines).
- No deep nesting without justification; errors handled explicitly.
- No secrets in source; no stray `console.log` in production paths.
- New behavior has tests where the stack allows; ~80% coverage on touched logic when tooling exists.

## Subagents this project uses

| Subagent | Use for | Harness output |
|----------|---------|----------------|
| **code-reviewer** | TS/JS/Python quality; typecheck/lint/test gates | `feedback/<n>/code-review-i<i>.md` |
| **api-reviewer** | Route handlers, HTTP/MCP contracts | `api-review-i<i>.md` |
| **mcp-reviewer** | MCP tools, schemas, transport | `mcp-review-i<i>.md` |
| **security-reviewer** | OWASP, secrets, trust boundaries | `security-review-i<i>.md` |
| **database-reviewer** | Migrations, SQL, RLS, indexes | `database-review-i<i>.md` |
| **frontend-evaluator** | Live UI vs rubrics (Playwright) | `frontend-eval-i<i>.md` |
| **performance-evaluator** | Perf budgets, Lighthouse | `performance-eval-i<i>.md` |
| **doc-updater** | User docs **after** other reviewers PASS | `docs-review-i<i>.md` |

## Severity

| Level | Meaning | Action |
|-------|---------|--------|
| **CRITICAL** | Security or data-loss risk | **BLOCK** |
| **HIGH** | Bug or serious quality issue | **BLOCK** |
| **MEDIUM** | Maintainability | **INFO** |
| **LOW** | Style / minor | **NOTE** |

## Workflow

1. Understand the diff.
2. Run **security-reviewer** when security triggers match.
3. Run **database-reviewer** when SQL/migrations/schema/RLS change.
4. Run **api-reviewer** / **mcp-reviewer** when HTTP/MCP contracts change.
5. Run **code-reviewer** for substantive code.
6. Run tests per `docs/project/setup.md`.

## Approval

- **Approve** — no CRITICAL or HIGH open
- **Warning** — HIGH only, merge with caution
- **Block** — CRITICAL present

Flag PRs with architectural change and no linked ADR. User-facing doc drift: **doc-updater** after PASS.